:"success","api_error_status":null,"result":"{\"grade\":\"partial\",\"rationale\":\"Strengths: The answer separates one-time launch costs (legal/registration, listing, index licensing, platform onboarding, distribution setup) from recurring ones (administration, custody, transfer agency, index fees, listing, audit, tax, compliance, platform charges). It correctly says seed capital is contributed fund assets, not an expense. It explains that under a unitary fee the sponsor generally pays covered operating costs, while exclusions such as brokerage, taxes and extraordinary items fall to the fund. It also notes that investors bear trading costs, spreads and their own platform charges. It rightly treats allocation as contractual and avoids inventing a vendor quote or a universal dollar range. Omissions and weaknesses: (1) The break-even explanation is under-specified. It says only that fee revenue (rate × assets) must cover sponsor-borne costs. It never separates fixed from variable costs to give break-even assets ≈ annual fixed costs ÷ (retained fee rate − variable cost rate). It also never states that a break-even level exists only when that margin is positive. Its line that a 'positive retained margin requires revenue to exceed those costs' restates profitability rather than the required condition. (2) It does not mention the Investment Company Act §14(a) $100,000 net-worth condition for an initial public offering. Nor does it make the point that this condition is neither a launch budget nor a commercial break-even level, which was the key legal anchor for separating seed assets from spending. The Lazard example of manager-borne organization costs and directly paid trustees is illustrative, and the general unitary-fee allocation content covers it adequately. Overall, the content is useful but has a substantive gap on the requested break-even mechanism.\"}","ttft_ms":3073,"type":"result","duration_ms":8721,"uuid":"47972815-c6be-41aa-9786-acc2968c74a6","ttft_stream_ms":537,"time_to_request_ms":62,"first_content_frame_ms":537,"queued_turn_count":0,"result_index":0,"credential_expires_at":"2026-11-28T06:36:39.484Z"}
